{"kind":"artifact","text":"The repository's own remote is carried to the browser only when it is safe to link, decided by an allowlist. Exact clean pushed head 95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb on request/repo-remote-link; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 59b9a2c0, checkout clean, main untouched at 02558e8d. Under planner's dd883da0 and my promise 29b95948.\n\nWHAT THIS PATH CHANGES. LocalRepo gains a Remote field, populated by LocalWorktrees through the existing eight-second cache. gitRemotes reads every configured remote with git config --null --get-regexp, so a remote URL containing whitespace or newlines cannot split a record. linkableRemote picks the remote a reader would follow and admits it only if it is safe; webRemoteURL is the allowlist itself.\n\nTHE SELECTION RULE, AND THE ORDER IT RUNS IN, WHICH MATTERS MORE THAN THE RULE. origin if the repository has one, otherwise the lexicographically first remote name. Selection happens by NAME BEFORE the scheme filter, deliberately: if origin is an ssh URL and a sibling remote is https, the result is no link at all rather than a link to the sibling. A top bar that silently points somewhere other than the repository's own origin would be worse than a top bar that links nothing. With no remotes the field is empty and the JSON key is absent, which the browser reads as \"render the path exactly as today\".\n\nTHE ALLOWLIST. Only http and https are admitted. Every other scheme — ssh, git, file, and anything nobody has thought of — is refused by not being on the list, rather than by appearing on a list of dangerous ones. Being unrecognised is itself a refusal. A remote carrying userinfo is declined outright rather than stripped, so a credential like https://x-access-token:SECRET@host/org/repo never enters the HTTP response body at all.\n\nA DEFECT IN MY OWN FIRST TESTS, FOUND BY MUTATION AND WORTH RECORDING. The first integration tests here were not allowlist-sensitive: mutating the Go allowlist left them green, because every case they carried was caught by a different rule — git@host:... fails to parse, and both ssh:// and the credential URL have userinfo. The case that only the scheme check can refuse is git://github.com/org/repo.git, which parses cleanly and carries no userinfo. It is now in all three integration sites. Without it the allowlist could have been deleted from this layer with the suite still passing, which is exactly the kind of green that reports coverage it does not have.\n\nTESTS. TestWebRemoteURLAdmitsOnlyHTTPAndHTTPS, TestLinkableRemoteSelectsOriginThenTheFirstNameAlphabetically, and TestLocalWorktreesCarriesOnlyALinkableRemote.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, from a run I watched exit rather than taken from the agent that wrote the code. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 35 packages, zero failures and zero cached; npm test --prefix ui 95 tests, 95 pass, 0 fail; gofmt -l . empty; go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0 with no output; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty, so internal/service/uidist is byte-reproduced; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb","path":"internal/app"}}